1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,634
The Andromeda Galaxy is approximately 2.5
million light-years from Earth, according

2
00:00:05,634 --> 00:00:09,979
to measurements published by NASA and the
European Space Agency.

3
00:00:09,979 --> 00:00:14,102
That figure comes from observing a
specific type of variable star called a

4
00:00:14,102 --> 00:00:18,336
Cepheid, whose brightness pulses at a rate
tied to its intrinsic luminosity.

5
00:00:18,336 --> 00:00:20,082
The distance is not a guess.

6
00:00:20,082 --> 00:00:25,321
It is a calculation based on comparing how
bright the star appears from Earth to how

7
00:00:25,321 --> 00:00:26,693
bright it actually is.

8
00:00:26,693 --> 00:00:31,416
What that distance does not tell you is
how scientists confirmed Andromeda is a

9
00:00:31,416 --> 00:00:35,899
separate galaxy at all, or how they
measure objects billions of light-years

10
00:00:35,899 --> 00:00:36,736
farther still.

11
00:00:36,736 --> 00:00:39,771
How do scientists know about other
galaxies?

12
00:00:39,771 --> 00:00:44,117
When you look at a galaxy, you are not
seeing the galaxy as it is now.

13
00:00:44,117 --> 00:00:49,136
You are seeing light that left that galaxy
millions or billions of years ago and has

14
00:00:49,136 --> 00:00:51,525
been traveling through space ever since.

15
00:00:51,525 --> 00:00:56,796
Light is electromagnetic radiation, and it
carries information in its wavelength,

16
00:00:56,796 --> 00:00:58,357
intensity, and spectrum.

17
00:00:58,357 --> 00:01:00,311
A telescope collects that light.

18
00:01:00,311 --> 00:01:05,072
A spectrograph splits it into its
component wavelengths, producing a

19
00:01:05,072 --> 00:01:10,035
That spectrum contains absorption and
emission lines corresponding to specific

20
00:01:10,035 --> 00:01:13,408
elements. Hydrogen produces a
characteristic pattern.

21
00:01:13,408 --> 00:01:17,974
So does helium, oxygen, and iron. The
pattern is a fingerprint.

22
00:01:17,974 --> 00:01:23,701
If you see hydrogen's lines in a galaxy's
spectrum, hydrogen is present in that

23
00:01:23,701 --> 00:01:24,208
galaxy.

24
00:01:24,208 --> 00:01:29,269
The method is called spectroscopy, and it
has been used since the mid-1800s.

25
00:01:29,269 --> 00:01:33,620
The reason this works is that every
element absorbs and emits light at

26
00:01:33,620 --> 00:01:38,032
specific wavelengths, determined by the
energy levels of its electrons.

27
00:01:38,032 --> 00:01:40,118
Those wavelengths do not change.

28
00:01:40,118 --> 00:01:45,529
Hydrogen in a laboratory on Earth produces
the same spectral lines as hydrogen in a

29
00:01:45,529 --> 00:01:47,811
galaxy 10 million light-years away.

30
00:01:47,811 --> 00:01:52,621
The lines may be shifted in wavelength due
to the galaxy's motion, but the pattern

31
00:01:52,621 --> 00:01:53,853
remains recognizable.

32
00:01:53,853 --> 00:01:56,681
This is not interpretation. It is
measurement.

33
00:01:56,681 --> 00:02:01,844
The spectrum is a physical record, and the
lines either match known elements or they

34
00:02:01,844 --> 00:02:02,275
do not.

35
00:02:02,275 --> 00:02:08,136
Knowing a galaxy exists is not the same as
knowing how far away it is.

36
00:02:08,136 --> 00:02:12,683
Distance measurements rely on standard
candles, objects whose intrinsic

37
00:02:12,683 --> 00:02:15,309
brightness is known or can be calculated.

38
00:02:15,309 --> 00:02:17,251
Cepheid variables are one type.

39
00:02:17,251 --> 00:02:21,949
These are stars whose brightness
oscillates over a period of days or weeks.

40
00:02:21,949 --> 00:02:26,882
In 1908, Henrietta Swan Leavitt, working
at Harvard College Observatory, discovered

41
00:02:26,882 --> 00:02:31,576
that the period of a Cepheid's pulsation
is directly related to its luminosity.

42
00:02:31,576 --> 00:02:35,740
A Cepheid that pulses every 10 days is
intrinsically brighter than one that

43
00:02:35,740 --> 00:02:36,851
pulses every 3 days.

44
00:02:36,851 --> 00:02:39,796
If you measure the period, you know the
luminosity.

45
00:02:39,796 --> 00:02:44,531
If you know the luminosity and measure the
apparent brightness from Earth, you can

46
00:02:44,531 --> 00:02:45,859
calculate the distance.

47
00:02:45,859 --> 00:02:50,857
This is how Edwin Hubble, using the
100-inch Hooker Telescope at Mount Wilson

48
00:02:50,857 --> 00:02:55,791
Observatory, determined in 1923 that
Andromeda was far outside our own Milky

49
00:02:55,791 --> 00:02:56,051
Way.

50
00:02:56,051 --> 00:03:01,852
He identified Cepheids in Andromeda,
measured their periods, calculated their

51
00:03:01,852 --> 00:03:07,427
luminosities, and derived a distance of
approximately 900,000 light-years.

52
00:03:07,427 --> 00:03:12,486
That figure was later revised upward as
the Cepheid calibration improved.

53
00:03:12,486 --> 00:03:17,407
The current accepted distance is
approximately 2.5 million light-years.

54
00:03:17,407 --> 00:03:22,120
The method has not changed. The
calibration has become more precise.

55
00:03:22,120 --> 00:03:26,669
Cepheids are visible only out to a certain
distance, roughly 10 million to 100

56
00:03:26,669 --> 00:03:29,411
million light-years depending on the
telescope.

57
00:03:29,411 --> 00:03:31,389
Beyond that, they are too faint.

58
00:03:31,389 --> 00:03:34,973
For greater distances, astronomers use
Type Ia supernovae.

59
00:03:34,973 --> 00:03:39,794
These are thermonuclear explosions of
white dwarf stars that reach a consistent

60
00:03:39,794 --> 00:03:40,771
peak brightness.

61
00:03:40,771 --> 00:03:43,226
The mechanism is well understood.

62
00:03:43,226 --> 00:03:49,103
A white dwarf in a binary system accretes
matter from a companion star until it

63
00:03:49,103 --> 00:03:53,864
reaches approximately 1.4 solar masses,
the Chandrasekhar limit.

64
00:03:53,864 --> 00:03:59,284
At that mass, carbon fusion ignites
throughout the star nearly simultaneously,

65
00:03:59,284 --> 00:04:00,813
and the star explodes.

66
00:04:00,813 --> 00:04:05,721
Because the mass at detonation is always
near the same limit, the peak brightness

67
00:04:05,721 --> 00:04:07,539
is always near the same value.

68
00:04:07,539 --> 00:04:12,323
Type Ia supernovae have been detected in
galaxies billions of light-years away.

69
00:04:12,323 --> 00:04:16,932
When a galaxy is moving away from Earth,
the wavelengths of light it emits are

70
00:04:16,932 --> 00:04:17,523
stretched.

71
00:04:17,523 --> 00:04:22,395
This is called redshift. The faster the
recession, the greater the stretch.

72
00:04:22,395 --> 00:04:27,237
Redshift is measured by comparing the
observed wavelength of a spectral line to

73
00:04:27,237 --> 00:04:29,995
the wavelength that line has in a
laboratory.

74
00:04:29,995 --> 00:04:36,575
If hydrogen's red line, normally at 656.3
nanometers, appears at 660 nanometers in a

75
00:04:36,575 --> 00:04:39,944
galaxy's spectrum, that galaxy is
receding.

76
00:04:39,944 --> 00:04:42,487
The amount of shift tells you the
velocity.

77
00:04:42,487 --> 00:04:46,921
This is the Doppler effect for light,
analogous to the change in pitch of a

78
00:04:46,921 --> 00:04:48,813
siren as it moves away from you.

79
00:04:48,813 --> 00:04:54,160
In 1929, Edwin Hubble published a paper
showing that more distant galaxies have

80
00:04:54,160 --> 00:04:55,379
greater redshifts.

81
00:04:55,379 --> 00:04:59,196
The relationship is linear: velocity
increases with distance.

82
00:04:59,196 --> 00:05:02,200
This is evidence that the universe is
expanding.

83
00:05:02,200 --> 00:05:06,835
The slope of that relationship is called
the Hubble constant, and its value has

84
00:05:06,835 --> 00:05:08,360
been refined over decades.

85
00:05:08,360 --> 00:05:13,674
The Hubble Space Telescope, launched in
1990, has measured Cepheids in galaxies up

86
00:05:13,674 --> 00:05:17,432
to 60 million light-years away, improving
the calibration.

87
00:05:17,432 --> 00:05:22,918
The Planck satellite, which observed the
cosmic microwave background from 2009 to

88
00:05:22,918 --> 00:05:27,253
2013, provided an independent measurement
of the expansion rate.

89
00:05:27,253 --> 00:05:32,389
The two methods give slightly different
values, a tension that remains unresolved,

90
00:05:32,389 --> 00:05:37,150
but both confirm that distant galaxies are
receding and that the universe is

91
00:05:37,150 --> 00:05:37,776
expanding.

92
00:05:37,776 --> 00:05:39,266
Science is a process.

93
00:05:39,266 --> 00:05:44,021
It is not a person behind a curtain
handing down permanent answers.

94
00:05:44,021 --> 00:05:47,342
Early telescopes showed galaxies as fuzzy
patches.

95
00:05:47,342 --> 00:05:52,323
William Herschel, in the late 1700s,
cataloged thousands of nebulae, but he

96
00:05:52,323 --> 00:05:57,437
could not determine whether they were
clouds within the Milky Way or separate

97
00:05:57,437 --> 00:05:57,968
systems.

98
00:05:57,968 --> 00:06:00,187
The resolution was insufficient.

99
00:06:00,187 --> 00:06:04,139
By the early 1900s, larger telescopes
revealed structure.

100
00:06:04,139 --> 00:06:08,184
Spiral arms, central bulges, and dust
lanes became visible.

101
00:06:08,184 --> 00:06:13,738
The 100-inch Hooker Telescope, completed
in 1917, was the instrument that allowed

102
00:06:13,738 --> 00:06:17,029
Hubble to resolve individual stars in
Andromeda.

103
00:06:17,029 --> 00:06:21,558
Once individual stars were visible,
Cepheids could be identified, and distance

104
00:06:21,558 --> 00:06:22,603
could be measured.

105
00:06:22,603 --> 00:06:26,372
Modern telescopes resolve galaxies in
extraordinary detail.

106
00:06:26,372 --> 00:06:31,226
The Hubble Space Telescope has imaged
galaxies billions of light-years away,

107
00:06:31,226 --> 00:06:36,145
showing their shapes, star-forming
regions, and interactions with neighboring

108
00:06:36,145 --> 00:06:36,720
galaxies.

109
00:06:36,720 --> 00:06:41,928
The Sloan Digital Sky Survey, which began
in 2000, has obtained spectra for millions

110
00:06:41,928 --> 00:06:45,152
of galaxies, mapping their distances and
velocities.

111
00:06:45,152 --> 00:06:50,539
The James Webb Space Telescope, launched
in 2021, observes in infrared, penetrating

112
00:06:50,539 --> 00:06:55,926
dust and detecting galaxies formed within
a few hundred million years after the Big

113
00:06:55,926 --> 00:06:56,251
Bang.

114
00:06:56,251 --> 00:06:58,349
These are not artist's impressions.

115
00:06:58,349 --> 00:07:03,264
They are photographs, built from photons
collected over hours or days of exposure.

116
00:07:03,264 --> 00:07:07,781
The cosmic distance ladder is a sequence
of techniques, each building on the one

117
00:07:07,781 --> 00:07:08,176
before.

118
00:07:08,176 --> 00:07:13,188
Parallax, the apparent shift in a star's
position as Earth orbits the Sun, works

119
00:07:13,188 --> 00:07:15,883
for stars within a few hundred
light-years.

120
00:07:15,883 --> 00:07:20,050
Cepheids extend the ladder to tens of
millions of light-years.

121
00:07:20,050 --> 00:07:23,344
Type Ia supernovae reach billions of
light-years.

122
00:07:23,344 --> 00:07:27,110
Each method overlaps with the next,
allowing calibration.

123
00:07:27,110 --> 00:07:31,536
If Cepheids and supernovae are both
visible in the same galaxy, the

124
00:07:31,536 --> 00:07:36,160
supernova's brightness can be calibrated
against the Cepheid distance.

125
00:07:36,160 --> 00:07:40,091
The ladder is not a single measurement but
a series of cross-checks.

126
00:07:40,091 --> 00:07:44,321
The Gaia satellite, launched by the
European Space Agency in 2013, has

127
00:07:44,321 --> 00:07:48,430
measured parallaxes for more than a
billion stars with unprecedented

128
00:07:48,430 --> 00:07:49,035
precision.

129
00:07:49,035 --> 00:07:52,176
Those measurements anchor the bottom of
the ladder.

130
00:07:52,176 --> 00:07:56,304
Cepheids, calibrated against parallax
distances, anchor the middle.

131
00:07:56,304 --> 00:08:00,201
Supernovae, calibrated against Cepheids,
anchor the top.

132
00:08:00,201 --> 00:08:05,560
The entire structure depends on the
consistency of physical laws across space

133
00:08:05,560 --> 00:08:06,187
and time.

134
00:08:06,187 --> 00:08:10,837
If the speed of light varied, or if atomic
spectra changed, the ladder would

135
00:08:10,837 --> 00:08:13,957
collapse. No evidence suggests either has
occurred.

136
00:08:13,957 --> 00:08:19,056
The evidence for other galaxies is not a
single observation but a convergence of

137
00:08:19,056 --> 00:08:20,331
independent methods.

138
00:08:20,331 --> 00:08:25,521
Spectroscopy identifies composition.
Standard candles measure distance.

139
00:08:25,521 --> 00:08:29,541
Redshift measures velocity. Imaging
resolves structure.

140
00:08:29,541 --> 00:08:34,173
Each method has been tested, refined, and
cross-checked against others.

141
00:08:34,173 --> 00:08:36,000
The measurements are public.

142
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:41,173
The Hubble Space Telescope's archive
contains more than 100 terabytes of data,

143
00:08:41,173 --> 00:08:42,565
accessible to anyone.

144
00:08:42,565 --> 00:08:46,244
The Sloan Digital Sky Survey's data
releases are freely available.

145
00:08:46,244 --> 00:08:48,864
The evidence is not locked behind closed
doors.

146
00:08:48,864 --> 00:08:51,595
It is published, peer-reviewed, and
reproducible.

147
00:08:51,595 --> 00:08:56,427
What this evidence does not establish is
the ultimate fate of the universe, or

148
00:08:56,427 --> 00:09:00,144
whether other universes exist beyond the
observable horizon.

149
00:09:00,144 --> 00:09:04,778
The observable universe is limited by the
speed of light and the finite age of the

150
00:09:04,778 --> 00:09:05,173
cosmos.

151
00:09:05,173 --> 00:09:10,265
Light from objects beyond a certain
distance, accounting for expansion, has

152
00:09:10,265 --> 00:09:12,165
not had time to reach Earth.

153
00:09:12,165 --> 00:09:16,624
Anything beyond that distance is not
observable, and claims about it are not

154
00:09:16,624 --> 00:09:18,091
based on direct evidence.

155
00:09:18,091 --> 00:09:22,505
The methods described here apply only to
the observable universe.

156
00:09:22,505 --> 00:09:25,221
They say nothing about what lies beyond.

157
00:09:25,221 --> 00:09:30,629
Einstein's general relativity predicts
that mass warps spacetime, and light

158
00:09:30,629 --> 00:09:32,288
follows that curvature.

159
00:09:32,288 --> 00:09:35,221
When a massive galaxy or galaxy cluster
lies between Earth and a more distant

160
00:09:35,221 --> 00:09:37,392
galaxy, the foreground mass acts as a
gravitational lens.

161
00:09:37,392 --> 00:09:41,852
Light from the background galaxy bends
around the lens, often producing multiple

162
00:09:41,852 --> 00:09:43,915
images, arcs, or even complete rings.

163
00:09:43,915 --> 00:09:49,368
The effect was first observed in 1979 when
astronomers identified a double image of a

164
00:09:49,368 --> 00:09:54,372
distant quasar, later confirmed to be a
single object lensed by an intervening

165
00:09:54,372 --> 00:09:54,821
galaxy.

166
00:09:54,821 --> 00:09:59,808
The amount of bending depends on the mass
of the lens and the distances involved.

167
00:09:59,808 --> 00:10:03,821
By measuring the positions and
brightnesses of the lensed images,

168
00:10:03,821 --> 00:10:09,070
astronomers can calculate both the mass of
the lensing object and the distance to the

169
00:10:09,070 --> 00:10:10,181
background galaxy.

170
00:10:10,181 --> 00:10:14,656
The Hubble Space Telescope has cataloged
thousands of gravitational lenses.

171
00:10:14,656 --> 00:10:19,130
Some of the most distant galaxies ever
observed have been confirmed through

172
00:10:19,130 --> 00:10:21,397
lensing by foreground galaxy clusters.

173
00:10:21,397 --> 00:10:25,811
The lensing magnifies the background
galaxy, making it visible when it would

174
00:10:25,811 --> 00:10:27,728
otherwise be too faint to detect.

175
00:10:27,728 --> 00:10:32,838
This is not a distortion that obscures
information—it is a magnification that

176
00:10:32,838 --> 00:10:33,568
reveals it.

177
00:10:33,568 --> 00:10:37,620
The method is independent of standard
candles and redshift measurements,

178
00:10:37,620 --> 00:10:40,208
providing a cross-check on distance
estimates.

179
00:10:40,208 --> 00:10:46,395
NASA's Hubble archive contains images of
lensed galaxies at distances exceeding 13

180
00:10:46,395 --> 00:10:47,904
billion light-years.

181
00:10:47,904 --> 00:10:53,054
When astronomers measure the rotation
speed of stars and gas in a spiral galaxy,

182
00:10:53,054 --> 00:10:58,075
they find that objects far from the
galactic center move faster than expected.

183
00:10:58,075 --> 00:11:02,816
According to Newtonian mechanics, if most
of a galaxy's mass were concentrated in

184
00:11:02,816 --> 00:11:07,499
the visible stars and gas near the center,
objects farther out should orbit more

185
00:11:07,499 --> 00:11:12,416
slowly, just as outer planets in the solar
system orbit more slowly than inner ones.

186
00:11:12,416 --> 00:11:17,573
Instead, rotation speeds remain roughly
constant out to the edge of the galaxy.

187
00:11:17,573 --> 00:11:23,082
This was first documented in the 1970s by
Vera Rubin and Kent Ford at the Carnegie

188
00:11:23,082 --> 00:11:28,052
Institution, using spectrographic
observations of the Andromeda Galaxy and

189
00:11:28,052 --> 00:11:28,523
others.

190
00:11:28,523 --> 00:11:33,211
The discrepancy implies that galaxies
contain far more mass than is visible.

191
00:11:33,211 --> 00:11:38,311
Observations show that while visible
matter would predict rotation velocities

192
00:11:38,311 --> 00:11:43,479
dropping to perhaps 150 kilometers per
second at a galaxy's outer edge, actual

193
00:11:43,479 --> 00:11:48,447
measurements often show velocities
remaining near 220 kilometers per second

194
00:11:48,447 --> 00:11:49,109
or higher.

195
00:11:49,109 --> 00:11:51,721
This unseen mass is called dark matter.

196
00:11:51,721 --> 00:11:56,943
It does not emit, absorb, or reflect
light, but its gravitational influence is

197
00:11:56,943 --> 00:11:57,680
measurable.

198
00:11:57,680 --> 00:12:02,572
The rotation curves are evidence not of
what dark matter is, but that something

199
00:12:02,572 --> 00:12:03,872
with mass is present.

200
00:12:03,872 --> 00:12:08,642
Multiple independent observations support
this: gravitational lensing shows more

201
00:12:08,642 --> 00:12:13,173
mass than visible matter accounts for, and
the cosmic microwave background's

202
00:12:13,173 --> 00:12:17,823
temperature fluctuations require dark
matter to match the observed large-scale

203
00:12:17,823 --> 00:12:19,373
structure of the universe.

204
00:12:19,373 --> 00:12:23,547
The evidence converges, but the nature of
dark matter remains unknown.

205
00:12:23,547 --> 00:12:28,716
Other galaxies are known through light
that has traveled across space, carrying

206
00:12:28,716 --> 00:12:31,072
information that instruments decode.

207
00:12:31,072 --> 00:12:35,412
The distances are measured, the
compositions are identified, and the

208
00:12:35,412 --> 00:12:36,688
motions are tracked.

209
00:12:36,688 --> 00:12:41,576
The evidence is electromagnetic radiation,
analyzed and cross-checked.

210
00:12:41,576 --> 00:12:43,531
That is how scientists know.
